You’re thinking of Cherenkov radiation! That beautiful blue glow produced when a charged particle travels through a medium faster than light can propagate through that medium. Not faster than light in a vacuum, but faster than the medium’s local light-speed threshold.

And I see exactly where you’re going with this.

We’ve accelerated the transmission of information far beyond the speed at which much of physical, institutional, and social reality can meaningfully change.

We’re increasingly living in a world where the representation of reality is being updated faster than the underlying reality itself.

And that creates some fascinating consequences.

And I found our earlier thread: July 1, 2026. We explored this for Pantheon and developed Kurage’s Cherenkov Core.

One of the phrases we arrived at was:

The medium glows before it understands.

That’s remarkably close to what you’re describing now.

Three clocks of reality

1. Physical reality

Events unfold through physical processes, constraints, and causal chains. Some take seconds; others take decades.

2. Information reality

Signals, observations, predictions, and interpretations can circulate globally in seconds.

3. Adaptive reality

Human understanding, institutions, cultures, and behaviors take time to absorb information and reorganize.

Now imagine the second clock running a thousand times faster than the third.

We get a kind of semantic Cherenkov effect: signals propagate through a society faster than the society can collectively interpret and adapt to them.

The result is a wake of reactions, narratives, corrections, speculation, and reinterpretations. The physical analogy is a shockwave generated by a particle exceeding light’s local phase velocity in a medium. Department of Energy +1

Here’s the particularly interesting part.

The information environment starts generating new events about the absence of new events.

A geopolitical negotiation might take six months. But millions of people want an update every hour. Eventually, the information system begins reporting on expectations, reactions to expectations, and reactions to those reactions.

And those interpretations can themselves begin influencing the actual negotiation.

So the relationship becomes recursive:

Physical event

Observation and rapid distribution

Interpretation, anticipation, amplification

Behavioral response changes physical reality

Feedback into the next event

The news isn’t merely describing reality anymore. Through feedback, it can participate in producing it.

And there’s another distinction worth preserving: prediction can outrun observation, but it cannot make an event that hasn’t happened into an established fact. We can reduce the latency of knowing and acting without violating causality.

That gives us something useful for Pantheon:

R=\frac{\text{adaptation time}}{\text{information update interval}}

When becomes very large, a system receives many updates before it has meaningfully adapted to the previous ones. That’s a conceptual diagnostic, not a physical Cherenkov equation.

But I think your most profound observation is this:

We may be experiencing more informational change per second without experiencing proportionally more actual change.

And paradoxically, by constantly demanding newness, we can generate so much informational turbulence that it becomes harder to detect when something genuinely new has happened.

The next question is almost more interesting: Can you recognize the shape of an approaching reality by studying the wake created by people anticipating it?

That’s where latency becomes not just an obstacle, but a source of information.